1. What does the `:=` operator do in Go?
   A) Declares and initializes a variable.
   B) Declares a type alias.
   C) Compares two values.
   D) None of the above.

2. Which keyword is used to define a function in Go?
   A) func
   B) function
   C) def
   D) method

3. What is the zero value of a pointer in Go?
   A) nil
   B) 0
   C) undefined
   D) NaN

4. How can you manage package imports in Go?
   A) Import each package on a new line.
   B) Group them into a single import block.
   C) Either A or B
   D) None of the above

5. What is the default visibility of a variable or function starting with a lowercase letter in Go?
   A) Global
   B) Package-wide
   C) Block scope
   D) None of the above

6. Which of the following is true about Goroutines in Go?
   A) They are lightweight threads managed by the Go runtime.
   B) They are heavyweight processes that require manual management.
   C) They do not allow concurrent execution.
   D) None of the above.

7. What is a channel in Go?
   A) A way to organize code into manageable sections.
   B) A Go data structure that prevents race conditions.
   C) A mechanism for type-safe, synchronous message passing between Goroutines.
   D) An external library used for networking.

8. What does the `select` statement do in Go?
   A) It selects the fastest running Goroutine to execute.
   B) It is used to choose from multiple send/receive channel operations.
   C) It filters slices based on conditions.
   D) It is a statement for switching between data types.

9. Which of these best describes the `sync.Mutex` type?
   A) It sends signals between goroutines to indicate the status of data.
   B) It provides a mutual exclusion lock to protect shared data.
   C) It compiles synchronized code blocks into asynchronous blocks.
   D) It handles memory management for Goroutines.

10. Which is a common mistake when using Goroutines inside loops?
    A) Forgetting to pass loop variables as arguments to Goroutines.
    B) Using a select statement inside the loop.
    C) Creating a new channel in each iteration of the loop.
    D) None of the above.

11. What is a slice in Go?
    A) A dynamically-sized, flexible view into the elements of an array.
    B) A fixed-size array.
    C) A map data structure.
    D) A type of function.

12. How do you declare a map in Go?
    A) map[keyType]valueType{}
    B) map{keyType:valueType}
    C) map[keyType,valueType]
    D) new(map[keyType]valueType)

13. Which data structure would you use for implementing a LIFO (Last In, First Out) mechanism?
    A) Array
    B) Slice
    C) Map
    D) Stack

14. What is an interface in Go?
    A) A collection of method signatures that a type must implement.
    B) A specific type of function.
    C) A special data structure for organizing types.
    D) A package of related methods.

15. Which of these statements about struct tags in Go is correct?
    A) They provide metadata about the structure fields, interpretable by the Go compiler.
    B) They are comments that help document the code.
    C) They add properties to fields for reflection.
    D) They are ignored by the Go programming language.

16. What is reflection used for in Go?
    A) Optimizing code at compile time.
    B) Manipulating objects at runtime by knowing their type.
    C) Creating new types during runtime.
    D) Connecting Go code to databases.

17. How does Go handle garbage collection?
    A) Manual memory management is required.
    B) Automatic garbage collection.
    C) Using reference counters.
    D) None of the above.

18. Which of the following is a true statement about package initialization in Go?
    A) Packages need to be initialized manually in the main package.
    B) Initialization occurs automatically when a package is imported.
    C) A package is initialized after its main function is executed.
    D) Package initialization is optional.

19. What is the purpose of the `go mod` command?
    A) To modify existing Go code.
    B) To provide version management for Go modules.
    C) To compile Go modules into binary files.
    D) None of the above.

20. Which practice enhances code reusability in Go?
    A) Using short, non-descriptive variable names.
    B) Frequent use of global variables.
    C) Writing loosely coupled, modular functions.
    D) Copying and pasting code between projects.

21. How do you handle errors in Go?
    A) Using try/catch blocks.
    B) Returning an error as part of the function's return signature.
    C) Ignoring errors.
    D) Using panic and recover.

22. Which tool can be used to format Go code?
    A) gofmt
    B) GoFix
    C) GoLint
    D) GoPad

23. Which statement about dependency management in Go is accurate?
    A) Dependencies are managed manually in the source code.
    B) Go uses a centralized package manager like npm or pip.
    C) Dependencies can be managed using `go get` and the Go module system.
    D) All dependencies must be compiled into the codebase.

24. What does the `defer` keyword do?
    A) Delays the execution of a function until the surrounding function returns.
    B) Defers the execution of a Goroutine.
    C) Postpones variable initialization.
    D) None of the above.

25. What is an idiomatic way to iterate over elements in a slice in Go?
    A) Using a for loop with an index variable.
    B) Using a map function.
    C) Using pointers.
    D) Using a while loop.
